+        _desc = "Four-way pixel-smooth camera over a META_TILESET room. The room is flattened at compile time into a char plane and a colour plane, so the runtime never decodes stamps. JSR MSC_L/MSC_R/MSC_U/MSC_D to move the camera one pixel that way, and JSR MSC_Update every frame so a coarse step still finishes when nothing is moving. Clicking any of those five names on the node drops a ready-made JSR node for it. Fine scroll is $D016 for L/R and $D011 for U/D; the visible window is rows 1-23, cols 1-38, with the outer row and column left blank to hide the edges. Those edges are only ever mostly hidden - 38-column mode covers 7 pixels on the left and 24-row mode 4 at top and bottom, never a whole 8-pixel cell - so BLANK CH sets which character fills them, and it must be an empty glyph in your charset. It defaults to char 0 rather than $20, because $20 is a space only in the ROM charset and is usually a real glyph in a custom one. COLOUR MODE decides what happens to colour RAM. FIXED is the stock C64 setting: in multicolour text mode the three shared colours come from $D021/$D022/$D023 and the colour-RAM nibble only supplies the fourth, so a room drawn from one palette needs no colour scrolling at all - the nibble is written once at init, the coarse step costs a single frame and the colour plane is never built. SHIFT scrolls colour RAM per cell for maps that really do vary, splitting the coarse step over two frames - chars first, then colour - which is worth it on a C64 Ultimate in turbo but not on stock hardware.";
     } else if (_node.node_type == "MACRO_VSCROLL") {
         _desc = "Vertical map scroller using dual screen buffers at $0400 and $0C00. JSR Scroller_U each frame to scroll up, JSR Scroller_D to scroll down. Fine scroll via $D011 bits 0-2 shadow register, coarse step copies 24 rows into inactive buffer shifted by one row, loads new edge row, flips $D018. Safe to combine with MACRO_SCROLL - JSR both entry points in the same frame routine.";
     }
